                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "8e0711c4-6e51-3505-9a53-5d8bf7262972",
                        "entry_id": "dd49a290-6867-33e2-8dde-991fc8345adc",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A title of the Byzantine emperor."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "98a1a95c-3115-404f-a122-f97e33455bd8",
                                "sense_id": "8e0711c4-6e51-3505-9a53-5d8bf7262972",
                                "text": "For two centuries its towers and domes were to rise in the heavens, drawing the attention of Emperors and Basilei, of Popes and Patriarchs."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "9c0d3291-9f27-45dc-bffc-828eecacaba3",
                                "sense_id": "8e0711c4-6e51-3505-9a53-5d8bf7262972",
                                "text": "Although portrayals of basileis of the eleventh century wearing the loros in the traditional manner are not lacking, I cannot cite a Byzantine example of metropolitan origin later than 1100."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "1b1523f3-55a3-43b7-a50b-d94734a10cc4",
                                "sense_id": "8e0711c4-6e51-3505-9a53-5d8bf7262972",
                                "text": "The revitalization of the Byzantine Empire during the reigns of the energetic and capable basilei of the Comnens dynasty: Alexios I (1081-1118), John II (1118-1143) and Manuel (1143-1180), and the wars with a Rum Sultanate troubled by dynastic fights drastically diminished its possessions in Anatolia."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "03d2a365-6dbb-4ac9-8e39-0b5deee9f992",
                                "sense_id": "8e0711c4-6e51-3505-9a53-5d8bf7262972",
                                "text": "It is quite easy to establish the identity of these “Roman princes” who reoccupied Pannonia after Attila’s death: they were the basilei from Constantinople who, after the fall of the Western Roman Empire, claimed to be the only successors of the Caesars (around the turn of the 9ᵗʰ century they began to call themselves “basilei of the Romans”—“basileis ton Romanon” or “Romaion”)."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0332641c-006f-4fed-a042-6745d4eb5c76",
                                "sense_id": "8e0711c4-6e51-3505-9a53-5d8bf7262972",
                                "text": "In our third example, the tyrant Nabis usurped power from the traditional, dual basileis of Sparta, then had them executed."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0ccb6a3b-7a20-46ae-a208-dd6d1d57f5ab",
                                "sense_id": "8e0711c4-6e51-3505-9a53-5d8bf7262972",
                                "text": "The basileis of the Iron Age were now replaced with various appointed and elected magistrates."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "6b0bce2d-fddb-4060-9cb6-19b786a89523",
                                "sense_id": "8e0711c4-6e51-3505-9a53-5d8bf7262972",
                                "text": "It would probably be worth seeking a possible connection-and-overlay between the name of one of the Byzantine basilei of the 9th century and the godfather of Boris I, Michael III, and that of the Bulgarian ruler Boris-Michael himself, who also lived in that age."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "2443b5c6-226a-4aec-82a5-4cf793310025",
                                "sense_id": "8e0711c4-6e51-3505-9a53-5d8bf7262972",
                                "text": "“Peter and Maria basilei of Bugaria,” “Peter and Maria in Christ autocrats of the Bulgarians,” and “Peter and Maria in Christ august autocrats and basilei.”"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
